---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';

const confidenceLevels = [
  { level: 'Confirmed', meaning: 'Reproduced, or stated by a primary source with direct evidence.' },
  { level: 'Likely', meaning: 'Consistent across several independent sources, with no contradicting evidence.' },
  { level: 'Possible', meaning: 'Plausible and partly supported. Evidence is limited or single-sourced.' },
  { level: 'Unverified', meaning: 'Reported but not checked. Kept only when the uncertainty is itself useful.' },
];

const sourceGrades = [
  { grade: 'A', source: 'Primary: vendor advisories, maintainer patches, original samples and telemetry we can inspect.' },
  { grade: 'B', source: 'Established secondary: reputable research teams and security agencies that cite their evidence.' },
  { grade: 'C', source: 'Other secondary: news coverage and commentary. Used for context, not for key claims.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Research methodology" description="How CyberPulse Intelligence researches, grades sources, states confidence and handles indicators of compromise." path="/methodology/">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Methodology"
    title="How we research"
    description="The process behind every article: how we gather evidence, grade sources, state confidence and handle indicators. This describes the intended process for the project."
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 pb-24 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose-article">
      <h2>1. Scope and questions</h2>
      <p>
        Every article starts with a written question, such as "Is this vulnerability exploitable in a default
        configuration?" or "Which detections still work if the sample is repacked?" A piece that cannot state its
        question is not ready to publish.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Source grading</h2>
      <p>Sources are graded by how close they are to the evidence. Key claims should rest on grade A or B sources.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>Source type</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {sourceGrades.map((row) => (
              <tr><td>{row.grade}</td><td>{row.source}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>3. Confidence levels</h2>
      <p>Each substantive claim is tagged with one of four confidence levels. Where a claim is not tagged, it is background.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {confidenceLevels.map((row) => (
              <tr><td><strong>{row.level}</strong></td><td>{row.meaning}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>4. Indicators of compromise</h2>
      <p>
        Indicators are published only when they help a defender and only with context on how long they remain
        useful. Example and reserved values are used in documentation. Where possible, we publish indicators in
        a machine-readable form next to the article, with a last-seen date.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Mark every indicator with its confidence level.</li>
        <li>Give a review date. Stale indicators are retired, not left in place.</li>
        <li>Never publish personal data found in samples or telemetry.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Exploit and vulnerability content</h2>
      <p>
        We write about exploitation for defenders: how it is observed, how exposure is checked and what changes
        the risk. We do not publish working exploit code or step-by-step instructions for attacking systems.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Review, corrections and labelling</h2>
      <p>
        Articles are reviewed by a second person before publication. When a published claim is found to be wrong,
        we correct the article and set its <em>Updated</em> date. Material corrections are noted at the top of
        the article.
      </p>
      <p>
        Anything that is not real research is labelled as sample content. This includes placeholder CVE
        identifiers, illustrative examples and template articles. Sample articles can never be cited as findings.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Reproducibility</h2>
      <p>
        Where a technique can be reproduced safely, the article describes how to reproduce it, using lab
        resources and clearly marked example data. Where it cannot be shared safely, the article says so.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
